## Start here

Read these in order. This page uses Effect 4 names.

1. [Effect documentation](https://effect.website/docs): start with concepts; choose Effect 4 examples.
2. [Services and Layers](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/04-services-and-layers.md): separate the service contract from its implementation.
3. [Data Modeling](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/05-data-modeling.md): use Schema for values crossing boundaries.
4. [Error Handling](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/06-error-handling.md): distinguish expected failures from defects. Check API spellings against your installed version.
5. [Testing](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/08-testing.md): provide test services with `@effect/vitest`.

## Answer both structure questions

[Justin Bennett asks about folders and service organization](https://x.com/just_be_dev/status/2107194260860613059), including `src/platform`.
[Sam Goodwin recommends](https://x.com/samgoodwin89/status/2107204053151428850):

> src/{domain} instead of src/{mechanism}. E.g. src/coding/edit.ts instead of src/tools/edit.ts. "tools" is the mechanism, "coding" is the domain.

Sam also recommends one service plus Layer per file, and one entrypoint plus `Effect.provide` per file.
We use that shape. It is a recommendation, not an Effect folder requirement.

## The shape in one tree

This is a small-app sketch, not a required template.

```text
src/
├── files/
│   ├── file-inspector.ts    Service, dependency capture, Layer
│   ├── stats.ts             Schema and expected errors
│   └── inspect-machine.ts   Optional finite lifecycle
├── platform/
│   └── files-node.ts        Platform adapter Layer
└── main.ts                  Composition root and runtime entrypoint
test/
└── files/
    └── file-inspector.test.ts
```

**What to notice:** Domain folders own behavior. `platform` supplies implementations, not domain rules.

A **composition root** is the entrypoint module that assembles and provides the application's Layers.
Use one per entrypoint, not one global root for every executable.

`src/platform` is fine for adapters behind a port: a service interface that names the application's job.
Domain code uses that interface; the composition root chooses the platform implementation.
rat-stack separates these jobs into packages. A small app need not copy its workspace layout.

## The rules that hold it together

### Capture dependencies when constructing a service

[Effect's service guidance](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/effect%404.0.0/packages/effect/AGENTS.md) colocates `Context.Service` and its Layer.
Our `FileInspector` captures `FileSystem` during construction. Its methods have no filesystem requirement.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/file-inspector.ts at=54d356037c994f89698760a4727be71d0005a087 lines=6-16,33-35
```

**What to notice:** Construction needs the dependency; callers need only `FileInspector`.
See [the Layer constructor pattern](/lore/layer-constructor-pattern).

### Decode at boundaries with Schema

[Data Modeling](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/05-data-modeling.md) uses Schema for runtime validation and static types.
rat-stack's `packages/core/src/contracts.ts` owns input, output, and failure schemas.
See [schemas define the boundary](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary).

### Keep expected failures typed and defects distinct

[Error Handling](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/06-error-handling.md) separates expected failures from unexpected defects.
`FileInspector` declares `FileStatsError` and preserves the failed path and filesystem reason.
See [the error model](/lore/error-model). A crash is not a domain refusal.

### Provide Layers at the entrypoint

[Effect's guidance](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect/blob/effect%404.0.0/packages/effect/AGENTS.md) composes focused Layers with `Layer.provide` and `Layer.provideMerge`.
Our CLI assembles dependencies before running the program.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/cli/src/cli.ts at=54d356037c994f89698760a4727be71d0005a087 lines=16-25
```

**What to notice:** `Effect.provide` resolves requirements before `NodeRuntime.runMain` runs the final Effect.

### Keep ports inside and adapters outside

[Services and Layers](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/04-services-and-layers.md) separates contracts from implementations.
Our adapter placement is house architecture: core owns `SubscriberIntake`; `packages/subscriber-delivery` implements it.
Apps provide that implementation. See [hexagonal architecture](/lore/hexagonal-architecture).

### Use machines for finite lifecycles

This is rat-stack policy, not an Effect requirement.
`packages/core/src/inspect-machine.ts` declares side effects with `fromEffect` and starts them with `createEffectActor`.
Use this for finite modes, cancellation, or legal event sequences. Keep direct Effects when those concerns do not apply.

### Test through Layers

[Testing](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/08-testing.md) introduces `it.effect` and scoped test services.
[Services and Layers](https://www.effect.solutions/services-and-layers) recommends fresh test Layers by default; share expensive resources with `it.layer`.
Our `packages/core/test/file-inspector.test.ts` provides filesystem services through `it.layer(TestLayer)`.

## House style versus Effect idiom

| Take the Effect idiom | rat-stack adds |
| --- | --- |
| Services and composable Layers | Colocated `make` and `static layer` |
| Schema-backed boundaries | Capability contracts and derived surface projections |
| Replaceable implementations | Cartridges with an add/remove test |
| Typed failures and scoped effects | XState lifecycles through `@xstate/effect` |
| Effect-aware tests | Property/model testing and a manual-runtime ban |
| Explicit requirements | A type-aware lint fence |
| No prescribed comment policy | No prose comments in code |

**What to notice:** You can use Effect without capability projections, cartridges, XState, or our lint rules.

## Common beginner mistakes

- **Choosing folders before jobs.** Name the domain first; put platform implementations behind its service interface.
- **Parsing unknown input by hand.** Decode it with Schema at the boundary.
- **Running an Effect inside another Effect.** Compose it instead; run at the integration edge.
- **Treating every failure as recoverable.** Keep expected errors typed; do not turn defects into ordinary outcomes.
- **Copying API names across versions.** Check your installed Effect guidance before copying examples.

## Where disagreement lives

- **Folder layout:** Sam favors domains. Effect does not mandate folders; Kit's [structure guide remains a draft](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/09-project-structure.md).
- **Routes:** Sam calls one API route per file potentially controversial. rat-stack instead keeps one capability per file and derives routes.
- **Mocks:** Test Layers express replaceable boundaries. The cited guidance does not establish that every mock is wrong.
- **Runners:** Domain code composes Effects. [Incremental adoption](https://github.com/kitlangton/effect-solutions/blob/main/packages/website/docs/10-incremental-adoption.md) uses runners to bridge existing imperative code.

These sources do not settle monorepo versus single-package layout. Choose that separately from the service graph.

